\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Real and complex algebraic states}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{Let #{K=\mathbb R} or #{K=\mathbb C}, with conjugation the identity
in the real case. Let #{A} be a unital associative #{K}-algebra equipped
with an additive operation #{a\mapsto a^{\dagger}} satisfying
##{\begin{aligned}
(\lambda a)^{\dagger}&=\overline\lambda a^{\dagger},&
(ab)^{\dagger}&=b^{\dagger}a^{\dagger},\\
(a^{\dagger})^{\dagger}&=a,&1^{\dagger}&=1.
\end{aligned}}
There is no graded sign in the product law. A #{K}-linear functional
#{\omega:A\to K} is \newvocab{Hermitian positive} when
##{\omega(a^{\dagger})=\overline{\omega(a)},\qquad
\omega(a^{\dagger}a)\in\mathbb R_{\geq0}\quad(a\in A).}
A \newvocab{real algebraic state}, respectively a
\newvocab{complex algebraic state}, is such a functional that is also
\vocabk{normalized}{fdip-0021}. The scalar field and involution are part of
the assertion.}

\p{Over #{\mathbb C}, nonnegativity on all #{a^{\dagger}a} already implies
Hermiticity, without assuming normalization. Over #{\mathbb R},
Hermiticity is a separate requirement, as the
[real dual-number example](fdip-0025) shows.}
\proof{
\p{Suppose #{K=\mathbb C} and #{\omega(a^{\dagger}a)\in\mathbb R_{\geq0}}
for every #{a}. For #{h=h^{\dagger}}, expansion and linearity give
##{\begin{aligned}
4\omega(h)={}&\omega((1+h)^{\dagger}(1+h))\\
&-\omega((1-h)^{\dagger}(1-h))\in\mathbb R.
\end{aligned}}
For arbitrary #{a}, put #{h=(a+a^{\dagger})/2} and #{k=(a-a^{\dagger})/(2\ui)}.
Conjugate scalar compatibility gives #{h=h^{\dagger}}, #{k=k^{\dagger}},
#{a=h+\ui k} and #{a^{\dagger}=h-\ui k}. The preceding calculation makes
#{\omega(h)} and #{\omega(k)} real. Hence
#{\omega(a^{\dagger})=\omega(h)-\ui\omega(k)=\overline{\omega(a)}}.}
}
\p{For these conventions, see
\citet{Section 3.1, p. 9; Definition 3.7 and Definition 3.9 with Remark 3.10(iv), p. 11}{waldmann2004states}.
Waldmann works over a complexification of an ordered ring. Here the
coefficients are the ordinary fields #{\mathbb R} and #{\mathbb C}; the
real Hermiticity requirement is explicit. On p. 9, Definition 3.1 prints
#{R=-P\sqcup\{0\}\sqcup R}; the intended final #{P}, rather than the
printed #{R}, is a mathematical emendation, not a verbatim transcription.}

\p{The [ordinary linear involution](fdip-001G) is not a substitute for
conjugate scalar compatibility over #{\mathbb C}. With a complex-linear
unital involution, #{(\ui 1)^{\dagger}(\ui 1)=-1}; a normalized functional takes this
to #{-1}, contradicting square positivity. The earlier
[chosen bilinear adjoints](fdip-001P) likewise do not supply this Hermitian
structure. No norm, continuity or operator representation is assumed.}
